      "text": "The largest prime divisor of 11! + 12! can be found by first calculating 11! and 12!:\n\n11! = 1 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot ... \\cdot 10 \\cdot 11 = 39916800\n12! = 1 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot ... \\cdot 10 \\cdot 11 \\cdot 12 = 479001600\n\nNext, we find the sum of these two factorials:\n\n11! + 12! = 39916800 + 479001600 = 518918400\n\nNow, we need to find the largest prime divisor of 518918400. To do this, we can start by finding the prime factorization of 518918400.\n\nFirst, we check if 518918400 is divisible by 2:\n\n518918400 is even, so it is divisible by 2.\n\nTo find the number of times 2 divides 518918400, we can repeatedly divide by 2 until we get an odd number:\n\n518918400 / 2 = 259459200\n259459200 / 2 = 129729600\n129729600 / 2 = 64864800\n64864800 / 2 = 32432400\n32432400 / 2 = 16216200\n16216200 / 2 = 8108100\n8108100 / 2 = 4054050\n4054050 / 2 = 2027025\n\nAt this point, 2027025 is odd, so we stop. The prime factorization of 518918400 is:\n\n518918400 = 2^11 \\cdot 3^2 \\cdot 5^2 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 11 \\cdot 13\n\nThe largest prime divisor of 518918400 is the largest prime factor in its prime factorization, which is 13.\n\nTherefore, the largest prime divisor of 11! + 12! is \\boxed{13}.",
